assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2008
says       A write by a second thread to a page mapped PROT_READ generates
           SIGSEGV for that thread: its handler runs in that thread (seen
           through a thread-local flag), not in the main thread, with
           si_code SEGV_ACCERR and si_addr the address written.
note       Memory protection makes the SIGSEGV required (XSH 2.8.3.3).
           The handler leaves with siglongjmp(), which is async-signal-
           safe; returning normally from it would be undefined.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/001-fault-to-thread.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGUSR1 blocked in the main thread and unblocked in a second
           thread, kill() of SIGUSR1 to the process runs the handler once,
           in the second thread.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/002-process-to-one-thread.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGUSR1 blocked in both threads, a SIGUSR1 sent to the
           process stays pending (the handler does not run within 300 ms);
           when the second thread unblocks it, the handler runs in that
           thread.
note       pthread_sigmask() delivers at least one pending, unblocked
           signal before it returns, so the second thread sees its
           handler has run.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/003-stays-pending-process.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGUSR1 blocked (and SIGUSR2 not), a thread created with
           pthread_create() and the child of fork() each start with SIGUSR1
           blocked and SIGUSR2 not blocked.
nomutant   the mask a thread or child starts with is set by the kernel
           and the thread library
test       t/004-mask-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      1988
says       A SIGUSR1 sent while blocked with handler A installed, then
           delivered after the action is changed to handler B, runs B and
           not A.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/005-action-at-delivery.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.006
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2001
says       With SIGCONT and SIGTSTP caught and blocked, SIGCONT sent to the
           process stays pending until SIGTSTP is sent; then SIGCONT is no
           longer pending and SIGTSTP is.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/006-stop-discards-cont.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.007
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2001
says       With SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN, SIGTTOU and SIGCONT caught and blocked, the
           three stop signals sent to the process are pending until SIGCONT
           is sent; then none of them is pending and SIGCONT is.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/007-cont-discards-stop.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.008
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2001
says       A child that ignores SIGCONT and stops itself with SIGSTOP is
           continued by a SIGCONT sent to it.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/008-cont-ignored.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.1-signal-generation.009
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2001
says       A child that catches and blocks SIGCONT and stops itself with
           SIGSTOP is continued by a SIGCONT sent to it; SIGCONT is then
           pending, and its handler runs once the child unblocks it.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/009-cont-blocked.c
